Reasoning (brief): I tried to digitize the crease pattern automatically (Hough line extraction, then a planar graph, a least-squares flat-fold fit and a Reeb graph along the folded axis). The extracted graph still had many local errors (missed short creases and T-junctions), so the folded fit was not reliable. The tree below is a best estimate. It comes from the circle/river structure visible in the CP:
- large corner flaps at (0,1) and (1,1), and a corner flap at (0,0);
- an interior middle flap at (0.45,0.135), whose hinge contour ring sits at radius ~0.18;
- short edge flaps at (1,0.68), (0,0.40) and (0.457,1);
- a cluster of short flaps near (0.44,0.64)/(0.37,0.90).
The segment lengths are estimated from where hinges meet the paper boundary. Confidence is low.

